#!/usr/bin/env python3
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
"""
cros-fprintd - fprintd-compatible D-Bus service for ChromeOS fingerprint MCUs

Speaks the net.reactivated.Fprint D-Bus API, so GNOME Settings, gdm, pam_fprintd
(login, lock screen, sudo, polkit) and the fprintd-* tools work unchanged. The
hardware side talks to the FPMCU through /dev/cros_fp (the cros_ec chardev).

How it works (all of it proven with the fpprobe* programs):
  * Once per boot the MCU is given a 32-byte secret (the "seed"), kept in
    /var/lib/cros-fprintd/seed (root only).
  * Each user gets a 32-byte id = HMAC-SHA256(seed, "cros-fprintd user:" + name).
    Setting that id as the MCU's context wipes the templates in RAM, so before
    every enroll/verify the daemon sets the context and loads the user's stored
    templates (encrypted blobs in /var/lib/cros-fprintd/users/<user>/).
  * Enrolling and matching run on the MCU; progress and results arrive as MKBP
    fingerprint events read from /dev/cros_fp.
  * Matching updates the template inside the MCU, so after a session the daemon
    reads the templates back out and rewrites the files if the MCU says "dirty".

Access rule (instead of polkit): a caller may act for a user if it runs as that
user or as root. pam_fprintd under sudo/gdm runs as root; GNOME Settings runs as
the user. Template files never leave the machine and are only readable by root.

Tests run it on a private session bus with a simulated MCU: see tests/fake_daemon.py.
"""
